WEBVTT

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.846
Starting Monday, November 23,

00:00:22.846 --> 00:00:26.285
Berkeley Heights production
will mark every in-process

00:00:26.285 --> 00:00:29.723
tag for the process the
product actually goes through.

00:00:29.723 --> 00:00:34.091
We're doing it on a new blue
in-process tag made for this plant.

00:00:34.091 --> 00:00:36.958
The old general tag
is being retired here.

00:00:36.958 --> 00:00:40.236
Our last internal audit
found two problems.

00:00:40.236 --> 00:00:43.057
Some in-process tags were left blank,

00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:47.783
and the steps printed on them
didn't match the process we run.

00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:49.537
That's finding IA 5-26.

00:00:49.537 --> 00:00:54.797
The other finding, IA 1-26, was two
products stored on the same rack.

00:00:54.797 --> 00:00:56.340
Both fall under P-852,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.568
our Identification and
Traceability procedure.

00:00:59.568 --> 00:01:02.795
Quality asked for a tag
that fits our process,

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:06.443
and for everyone on the
floor to be retrained on it.

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:12.074
The general tag listed steps
from a different process.

00:01:12.074 --> 00:01:14.159
Drawing, pirn winding, setting,

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:17.992
rewinding and texturing don't
happen at Berkeley Heights,

00:01:17.992 --> 00:01:21.220
so there was nothing true
to write next to them.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.487
The new tag lists our
four steps in order.

00:01:24.487 --> 00:01:27.909
Winding, twisting,
steaming, and inspection.

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:31.735
It's still blue, and it still
says In Process across the top.

00:01:31.735 --> 00:01:35.624
Below that is the space where
the finished product label goes.

00:01:35.624 --> 00:01:39.899
Each step has a box and a line
next to it. You'll use both.

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:43.414
When the product starts,

00:01:43.414 --> 00:01:47.138
apply the finished product
label from the order to the tag.

00:01:47.138 --> 00:01:50.735
You don't write the SA number
or the description by hand.

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:54.270
The label goes in the space
below the In Process header.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.750
When a step is finished,

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:59.634
check its box and write the date
and your initials on the line.

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:02.039
Winding is marked
when winding is done,

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:04.814
and twisting is marked
when twisting is done.

00:02:04.814 --> 00:02:07.181
Steaming happens in the POZZI.

00:02:07.181 --> 00:02:10.257
If the SA doesn't go
through the POZZI,

00:02:10.257 --> 00:02:12.702
write N/A on the Steaming line.

00:02:12.702 --> 00:02:16.410
The tag has no N/A box,
so write it in by hand.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.530
Mark a step only after it's done.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.449
A checked box tells the next
person the step really happened,

00:02:22.449 --> 00:02:24.954
and your initials
tell them who did it.

00:02:24.954 --> 00:02:27.643
On the production floor
at Berkeley Heights,

00:02:27.643 --> 00:02:29.538
you'll mostly use the blue tag,

00:02:29.538 --> 00:02:32.716
and it stays with the product
the whole way through,

00:02:32.716 --> 00:02:33.939
inspection included.

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:37.789
Yellow and green tags are used
only in a few limited processes,

00:02:37.789 --> 00:02:41.335
like marking pallets staged
for shipment in the warehouse,

00:02:41.335 --> 00:02:44.085
or marking that something
needs to be tested.

00:02:44.085 --> 00:02:49.124
One rack holds one SA, and the tag
on that rack matches what's on it.

00:02:49.124 --> 00:02:51.017
A tag is never left blank.

00:02:51.017 --> 00:02:55.969
If you find product with no tag, a
blank tag, or an old general tag,

00:02:55.969 --> 00:02:59.318
tell your supervisor
before the product moves.

00:02:59.318 --> 00:03:03.613
Do the same if the label doesn't
match the product on the rack,

00:03:03.613 --> 00:03:08.181
if one rack holds two SAs, or if
a step is marked that wasn't done.

00:03:08.181 --> 00:03:11.931
Don't guess, and don't fix
someone else's tag yourself.

00:03:13.931 --> 00:03:17.110
Before you take the
knowledge test, try these.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:24.245
A rack has product from two
SAs on it. What do you do?

00:03:24.245 --> 00:03:29.918
Stop, and tell your supervisor before
it moves. One rack holds one SA.

00:03:29.918 --> 00:03:36.753
Winding is finished on your
SA. What goes on the tag?

00:03:36.753 --> 00:03:40.988
Check the Winding box, and write the
date and your initials on the line.

00:03:40.988 --> 00:03:47.025
The label on the tag doesn't
match the product on the rack.

00:03:47.025 --> 00:03:48.560
What do you do?

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.661
Stop, and tell your supervisor before
it moves. Don't correct it yourself.

00:03:53.661 --> 00:03:58.135
Starting Monday, November 23,
every in-process tag at Berkeley

00:03:58.135 --> 00:04:00.950
Heights is marked
for the real process.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:04.261
Apply the finished product
label from the order.

00:04:04.261 --> 00:04:05.916
When a step is finished,

00:04:05.916 --> 00:04:09.295
check the box and add the
date and your initials.

00:04:09.295 --> 00:04:12.330
Write N/A on Steaming
when there's no POZZI.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.296
Keep the blue tag on
the whole way through,

00:04:15.296 --> 00:04:18.813
keep one SA to a rack, and
never leave a tag blank.

00:04:18.813 --> 00:04:21.756
Next, take the knowledge
test in ICF University

00:04:21.756 --> 00:04:24.136
and sign your training
acknowledgment.

00:04:24.136 --> 00:04:28.019
If you have a question about the
new tag, ask your supervisor.

